{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791122591,"nonce":"VoWWr6HWcj7ROvO0JteAsvBlXog=","title":"SSA: pooled @reference array literal slots overlap for structs with padding","message":"An array literal of @reference struct literals is placed in one pool (array_pool_placement, crates/compiler/src/ssa.rs, the pool built around the Array literal arm): slot_size = hir_ty_size(inner), which sums field sizes without padding (hir_ty_size, ssa.rs ~164). Each literal then lays its fields out with each field aligned to its own size and the total padded to the largest field (TypedExpression::Struct arm, ssa.rs ~6236), and writes at pool + i*slot_size. For a struct whose fields need padding, e.g. {a: i8, b: i64}, the slot is 9 bytes but the literal writes b at offset 8..16, so element i overwrites the start of element i+1. Any program building such an array literal corrupts its elements; structs whose fields are all the same width are unaffected, which is why nothing has shown it. Fix: size the slot with the same layout rule the literal uses (one shared helper for the literal, field read, field write and drop_glue::field_offsets), rounded to the struct's alignment.\n\nFound while mapping layouts for the host-heap M2 design; not reproduced yet.","files":null}]}